## Alphabet size

The size of the input alphabet is one of the key factors that influence the capacity of a transformer model. Some languages, such as Chinese and Russian, have larger alphabets than English because they do not have spaces between words, and thus all the characters (or even all the words) need to be encoded before the language model can process them. Larger alphabets imply that the model's embedding layer needs to have more dimensions to accommodate all possible entries. As a consequence, models trained on multilingual text with large alphabets will have larger input sizes and parameter counts, and thus require more computational resources and time during both training and inference.

On the other hand, models trained on languages with small alphabets can have smaller embedding dimensions, making them faster and more memory-efficient. For example, models trained for English can typically have a smaller number of parameters when compared to models trained for Chinese, because English uses a smaller alphabet with spaces between words, making it easier to encode and manage.

When working with a language that has a large alphabet, it's recommended to set the maximum sequence length to be smaller than the tokenizer's default, and to use a smaller batch size during training, especially when running on GPUs with limited memory.

## Tokenization

In natural language processing, tokenization is the process of breaking down a text into sequences of subwords or words, which can be processed by a machine learning model. For transformer models, pretraining tokenizers that come built-in with the models such as BERT, XLNet, RoBERTa, ALBERT, and DistilBERT must be carefully selected for the language of interest. Some tokenizers can handle multiple languages, but performance may vary depending on the language and the specific properties of each language, such as its alphabet size and character complexity.

Some transformer models that are designed to handle multilingual text (e.g., XLNet, ALBERT, mBERT) use byte-pair encoding, a text normalization method that automatically learns a mapping from words to subwords during pretraining. Byte-pair encoding (BPE) allows the model to tokenize texts with non-CJK scripts with spaces, like English, and texts without spaces, like Chinese, Arabic, and Urdu. This improves the model's generalization ability across languages with different alphabets and processing properties. For example, mBERT, which is a multilingual version of BERT, is trained on 104 languages and tokenizes texts using BPE. It is well-suited for multilingual NLP tasks.

It's important to note that even with tokenizers that work well for specific languages, errors may occur during tokenization, and these errors may have a negative impact on the performance of the transformer model. Fine-tuning the model specifically for the target language can help to reduce the tokenization errors and improve performance.

## Vocabulary size

Vocabulary size is another factor that affects transformer models, as it determines the maximum variation in the input string a model can process. A larger vocabulary size allows the model to learn a larger space of patterns and syntax, which can lead to better performance. However, a larger vocabulary size also increases the model's capacity, requiring more time and computational resources during pretraining, fine-tuning, and inference.

For transformer models trained on specific languages, it's important to ensure that the vocabulary size is reasonable and scalable. A well-designed vocabulary manager must efficiently handle the addition and removal of tokens, making the model more robust and easier to fine-tune. For example, methods like set-based Bloom filters, Trie-suffix arrays, and minimal perfect hash functions (MPH) can be used to manage the vocabulary and minimize data structure overhead during the training process.

