[94/200] Running: What is a sequence-to-sequence model?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 A sequence-to-sequence model is a type of deep learning architecture that is particularly well-suited for natural language processing tasks. It takes a sequence as input and generates a sequence as output. The key component of a sequence-to-sequence model is an encoder-decoder architecture, which processes the input sequence and generates the output sequence, respectively.

The encoder typically consists of a recurrent neural network (RNN) or a transformer, which reads the input sequence one token at a time, computes the hidden state, and passes it to the decoder. The decoder, on the other hand, also uses an RNN or transformer to generate tokens one by one. It starts with a special start token and at each time step it receives the hidden state from the encoder, predicts the next token, and then appends it to the output sequence.

Sequence-to-sequence models can be used for various tasks such as translation, speech recognition, text summarization, and machine translation.
