CREATE TABLE students (
    id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    name VARCHAR(255)
);
```

When you insert new students using an ORM or manual SQL, the database will automatically handle the ID generation for you.

```sql
INSERT INTO students (name) VALUES ('Student_1');
INSERT INTO students (name) VALUES ('Student_2');
```

For maximum safety and idempotency, you can use prepared statements with parameter binding and set the id column to the default value (0). This ensures that the ID generation is isolated and happens before the insert operation. In the case of a larger sequence, the operation should still be quick due to the auto-increment nature of the SERIAL type.

```sql
PREPARE insert_student (name VARCHAR(255)) AS
INSERT INTO students (name, id) VALUES (?, nextval('students_id_seq'));

EXECUTE insert_student ('Student_1');
EXECUTE insert_student ('Student_2');
```
