You're essentially doing a character-by-character comparison with the `requestHeaders`. This is an approach I've considered. But there's a risk that a mismatch isn't a legitimate sign the packet is malformed, it could just be a fluke. You'd do better to parse headers in order. And check if anything's missing at the end.

To implement this, you'd create a helper function:

```
bool is_header_complete(const char first, const char second, const char third) {
    if (shared_bytes[header_index] != first || shared_bytes[header_index + 1] != second || shared_bytes[header_index + 2] != third)
        return false;

    header_index += 3;
    return true;
}
```

Then you'd call something like this:

```
if (!is_header_complete('\\', '.', 'd'))
    throw std::runtime_error("Message not request header");

if (!is_header_complete('\x00', 'a', '2'))
    throw std::runtime_error("Message not request header");

...and so on.
```

The method I suggested here shouldn't cause any performance issues.

