After building a model, it's sometimes necessary to evaluate the model using different precisions, like 32-bit float, 16-bit float, and int8. To do this, use the `eval_type` parameter when calling Predictor's `apply()` function, as shown below.

Using float16:
```python
import tensorflow as tf
from tensorflow.keras.models import Sequential, Model
from tensorflow.keras.layers import Dense, Input, Add
import numpy as np

# Load the pre-trained model
model = tf.keras.models.load_model("my_model.h5")

# Define float16-only version of the model
with tf.keras.training.set_maximum_steps(0):
    input_1 = Input(shape=(10,))
    x = Dense(10, activation='relu', use_bias=True, kernel_initializer='he_normal')(input_1)
    output_1 = Dense(1)(x)
    model_float16 = Model(input_1, output_1)
    model_float16.compile(loss=tf.keras.losses.MeanSquaredError(), optimizer=tf.keras.optimizers.Adam())
    model_float16.set_weights(model.get_weights())

# Compile the model for float16 using the new float16 op-set
model_float16.compile(loss=tf.keras.losses.MeanSquaredError(), optimizer=tf.keras.optimizers.Adam(), run_eagerly=True, experimental_relax_shapes=True)

# Create input data
data = np.random.normal(size=(1000,10)).astype(np.float32)

# Compile and run with float16 precision
output_float16 = model_float16.apply(data, eval_type='float16')

```

